With $a=8$ and $b=16$:
$$\begin{aligned}f(x)&=-x^3+8x^2-20x+16\\&=(4-x)(x-2)^2\\g(x)&=\sqrt{x+3}+1.\end{aligned}$$
$f$: $f(0)=16$; double root at $x=2$ so the graph touches the $x$-axis at $(2,0)$; single root at $x=4$ so it crosses at $(4,0)$; $f\to+\infty$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $f\to-\infty$ as $x\to\infty$.

$g$: starts at $(-3,1)$, $g(0)=\sqrt3+1$, increasing.

Intersection: $f(1)=-1+8-20+16=3$ and $g(1)=\sqrt4+1=3$, so the graphs meet at $(1,3)$. This is the only intersection: for $-3\le x\le2$, $f$ is decreasing and $g$ is increasing, and for $x>2$, $f\le\frac{32}{27}<g$.

